From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ddraw: A windowed primary surface is presented once a refresh.

7860 shows a windowed (non-exclusive) DirectDraw primary through the swap chain -- the cure for
the VMR-7's frozen video on NVIDIA/Xwayland -- but presented the whole window after every Blt,
BltFast, Unlock, ReleaseDC or palette change. A program drawing many small things to the primary
(a sprite engine, a GDI-on-primary interface) paid one full present per draw: 200 32x32 Blts a
frame ran at 26 frames a second on an RTX 3090 and at 6 on llvmpipe (967's front buffer path:
25 and 23).

Windows' compositor shows a window's changes once a refresh, at the vertical blank, whatever was
drawn since; so does this. An update only marks the primary as waiting; a thread of ours
presents it at the next blank of the grid WaitForVerticalBlank already keeps (a frame every
1 / refresh rate), so a change shows within one refresh also when the program draws nothing
more. WaitForVerticalBlank presents at once (the program's frame is done), and so does the end
of the surface, of the swap chain or of the DirectDraw object. Reading the primary (Lock,
GetDC) needs none: it holds everything drawn to it. A present that meets the primary mapped
(between Lock and Unlock, GetDC and ReleaseDC) fails and the Unlock or ReleaseDC that follows
marks it again. The waiting objects are listed under the wined3d lock, which the thread takes
to present.

Same probe after: 527 frames a second (105000 Blts) on the RTX 3090, 431 (86000) on llvmpipe,
59 presents a second; GetDC + TextOut + ReleaseDC 1344 -> 7748 a second; whole-window Blts
5357 -> 75397. Gate test-ddperf; mutant SG_MUTANT_DDRAW_PRESENT_EACH_UPDATE.
